.TH CLRUCACHE_CREATE_FULL 3 "2026-09-17" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
clrucache_create_full \- create an LRU cache with full control
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/clrucache.h>

clru_cache clrucache_create_full(size_t capacity,
                                 ccol_data_type key_type,
                                 ccol_data_type val_type,
                                 clru_remote_getter_t getter,
                                 clru_remote_setter_t setter,
                                 clru_eviction_cb_t eviction_cb,
                                 ccol_memmgmt_procs_t *mprocs,
                                 char **err);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Creates a thread-safe Least Recently Used (LRU) cache, with O(1)
lookup through a hash map and O(1) eviction through a doubly-linked
list. The cache holds up to capacity live entries; an insert past that
limit first evicts the least recently used entry.

The library divides the cache into segments, each with its own lock,
and a key belongs to exactly one segment. Threads that work on keys in
different segments do not wait for one another, so the throughput
holds up as you add threads. Each segment evicts on its own; see
NOTES.

This is the low-level constructor; use the clru_construct() macro
family in application code instead.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
capacity     The maximum number of live entries before eviction
             happens. It must be > 0.
key_type     The ccol_data_type of the keys, as
             ccol_determine_ccol_data_type() returns it.
val_type     The ccol_data_type of the values. It serves only for type
             tracking, because the internal map always stores heap
             pointers.
getter       Remote getter callback, or NULL. The library calls it on
             a cache miss to fetch the value from an external source.
             On success it must allocate the value data on the heap,
             assign that pointer to val->ptr, set val->size to the byte
             length, and return true. The cache takes ownership of
             val->ptr and frees it with its own allocator, which is
             mprocs if the caller gives one and malloc()/free() if
             not. The allocation of the getter must use that same
             allocator, or the later free by the cache corrupts the
             heap. On failure the getter must leave val->ptr as NULL
             and return false.
setter       Remote setter callback, or NULL. The library calls it
             synchronously before each update of the cache, with the
             key and the value as const cmap_pair* pointers. When it
             returns false, the library does not update the cache.
             The library calls the getter and the setter with no lock
             of the cache held, so either one can use the cache for
             any other key. Neither must call back into the same cache
             for the key that it serves: a get or a set of that key
             waits for the operation that runs the callback, and the
             thread deadlocks against itself. Neither must destroy the
             cache, because the destroy waits for that same call.
eviction_cb  Eviction callback, or NULL. The library calls it when it
             evicts an entry to make room, and also for each entry that
             is in the cache when clru_destroy() runs. In both cases it
             holds the lock of the owning segment during the call. The
             callback gets the evicted key and value as const cmap_pair*
             pointers.
             It must not call back into the cache, and the library
             does not serialise it against itself; see NOTES.
mprocs       Custom memory management procedures, or NULL for the
             default malloc and free. The cache keeps a copy of the
             struct, so the caller may release or reuse the struct
             once this call returns, but the functions that it names
             must stay callable until the cache is destroyed.
err          An optional pointer that gets an error string on failure.
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
Returns a new cache handle on success.
Returns CLRU_CACHE_INVALID on failure, and sets *err when err is not
NULL.
CLRU_CACHE_INVALID is ((clru_cache)0), and plays the same role for a
clru_cache as NULL does for a pointer. Compare a handle directly
against it, or use the handle as a condition: if (!cache) is a correct
test for an invalid handle.
.fi
.SH "KEYS WITH PADDING"
.PP
A key of a type that the cache does not know (ccol_other_types, such as
a struct) is the same key as another one only when every byte of the two
is equal. Both the choice of segment and the internal map read every
byte of the key, and that includes the padding bytes of a struct. Two
keys with equal members but different padding are then two different
keys, and a lookup misses.
.PP
The cache takes no custom hash or key equality function, so the bytes of
a padded key must be fully determined. Through the raw functions, such
as clrucache_set_full(3), the cache reads exactly the object that
key_pair->ptr points at, so zero the whole key object with memset()
before you set its members. Through clru_get(3) and clru_set(3), the
cache sees the macro's own copy of the key, and C leaves the padding of
a struct copy unspecified. With GCC 11 or later the macros set that
padding to zero. With any other compiler, use the raw functions for a
padded key.
.SH NOTES
.nf
The handle that the function gives back is an opaque VALUE, not a
pointer. Do not cast it to void*, do not cast a void* to it, and do
not use it as an address.
The cache is thread-safe: per-segment locks and per-entry condition
variables protect every operation on the handle. Operations on the
same key still serialise against each other, and the coalescence of
concurrent misses for one key rests on that.

The division starts at a capacity of 128, the first capacity that
gives two segments 64 entries each. Below 128 a cache is a single
segment, and it evicts in one exact global least-recently-used order.

At 128 and above, each segment evicts on its own. The library divides
capacity across the segments, and each segment evicts its own least
recently used entry when its own share is full. The cache therefore
has the capacity that the caller requested, and clrucache_capacity()
reports exactly that number, but the entry that the library selects
to make room is the oldest inside one segment, not the oldest of the
whole cache. Because a hash spreads the keys, some segments run fuller
than others at any instant, so a cache that is full to exactly its
capacity holds a little less than that number. The shortfall is a few
percent: up to about ten below roughly a thousand entries, and around
two at eight thousand entries and above, falling further as the cache
grows. It depends neither on the key type nor on the width of the
build. The segment of a key comes from the keyed hash of chashmap,
which a secret of the process keys whatever the hash mode of the map
of each segment is, so the shortfall for one set of keys differs from
one process to the next. The map of each segment starts in the fast
hash mode and switches to the keyed mode on its own when the keys
that reach it collide; see chmap_create_full(3). Benchmark your
configuration against the access pattern that you intend instead of
assuming either behaviour.

The library calls the eviction callback while it holds the lock of the
owning segment, so the callback must not enter the cache again.
Because the library holds the lock of that one segment only, two
evictions in different segments run the callback at the same time on
two threads, and any state that the callback keeps of its own needs
its own protection.
clru_destroy() waits until every in-flight resolved use of the handle
stops before it frees anything. Such a use is a concurrent
clrucache_get_full(), clrucache_set_full(), clrucache_size() or
clrucache_capacity() call, so the caller does not need to prove on its
own that no other thread still uses the cache. A destroy of a handle
that a call already destroyed, whether in an earlier call or on
another thread that races this one, is a FATAL ERROR: it calls abort()
and raises SIGABRT, and is never a silent double-free.
.fi
